!.vscode/settings.json.example
.kotlin/
.classpath
.project
.settings/

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Local configuration and anything that could carry a credential.
# The pattern is deliberately wide: a file this repository does not name is not tracked.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.local.yml
*.local.yaml
*.local.properties
application-local.yml
application-local.yaml
application-secret*.yml
secrets/
*.pem
*.p12
*.jks
*.keystore
*-service-account*.json
credentials*.json

# Generated seed data -- see docs/decisions/publication-readiness.md PUB-7.
#
# THIS REPOSITORY TRACKS NO DATA FILE. The seed is produced by the generator in seed/,
# from a fixed seed value, so anyone can reproduce it. A committed dataset would be a
# dataset nobody could prove the provenance of, and the CI guard enforces the same rule
# from the other side so that a rename does not defeat it.
seed-out/
*.csv
*.tsv
*.jsonl
*.parquet
*.dump
*.bak
*.sql.gz

# Measurement output. Regenerated by the run that produced it; the NUMBERS live in
# docs/reports/, which is where a reader looks for them.
load/out/
*.gc.log
*.hprof
*.jfr

# Scratch
*.log
coverage/
.jacoco/
scratch/
tmp/

# `.study/` WAS IGNORED HERE AND IS NOT ANY MORE — removed 2026-08-14 on the PO's instruction.
#
#   It had two reasons. The first, that AGENTS.md requires repository documents to be in
#   English and those notes are Korean, is the PO's call and has been made.
#
#   The second was that this repository is public and `git add -A` is used routinely here, so
#   an untracked-but-unignored directory is one careless stage away from being published.
#   THAT RISK IS NOW SPENT: the PO took the second decision on 2026-08-17 and `.study/` is
#   tracked. Nothing there is one stage away from publication because all of it is published.
#
#   What replaces the habit: anything written under `.study/` from here on is written to be
#   read by strangers. Reading all seven files before that commit found four sentences that
#   would have become false the moment they were published -- three about the folder's own
#   tracking status, in the file that described the rule.
